And thanks again to them for supporting Vsauce2.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:39.240
Okay so.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:45.910
Spanish authorities actually knew Mayfield’s
print wasn’t a match -- before the FBI detained

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:46.910
him.

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:51.969
They communicated a result of “Negativo”
after their own fingerprint experts found

00:06:51.969 --> 00:06:54.659
too few points matching in the prints.

00:06:54.659 --> 00:06:56.080
So what’d the FBI do?

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.979
The FBI flew to Madrid and told them they
were wrong.

00:07:00.979 --> 00:07:05.610
As they investigated Mayfield, they found
that he’d converted to Islam.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:11.789
They also noted that a year prior to 11M,
he’d represented a man accused of involvement

00:07:11.789 --> 00:07:14.050
in a terror plot in Oregon.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:19.530
The more they looked into his life, the more
circumstantial evidence they found that suggested

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:25.520
Mayfield had the means and motive to play
a role in the attack -- and that’s actually

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:29.280
the punchline to a joke about a lying Texas
sharpshooter.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:34.710
The joke’s about a man who shoots at the
side of a barn, and then draws a target around

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:39.500
a cluster of bullet holes to suggest that
he’s actually a great shooter.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:44.530
When that’s done with data, it’s called
the “multiple comparisons problem.”

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:49.720
When we falsely perceive connections in our
heads, it’s “apophenia.”

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:55.360
And when you’re fitting evidence to a predetermined
conclusion, that has a name too.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:02.030
Psychologist Peter Wason coined the term “confirmation
bias” to describe one’s tendency to focus

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:07.889
on evidence that supports their beliefs and
to ignore or reject evidence that contradicts

00:08:07.889 --> 00:08:08.889
it.

00:08:08.889 --> 00:08:13.719
Basically, it’s cherry-picking the truth,
and it almost guarantees that you’ll find

00:08:13.719 --> 00:08:19.189
exactly what you’re looking for -- even
if that isn’t reality.

00:08:19.189 --> 00:08:24.300
SO WHAT WAS THE REALITY?

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:34.930
An FBI report years after the incident admitted
that Mayfield was actually one of 20 potential

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:37.839
matches to the LF-17 fingerprint.

00:08:37.839 --> 00:08:42.729
Once they looked into Mayfield’s life and
found details that supported their suspicions,

00:08:42.729 --> 00:08:45.190
he became the prime suspect.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:50.019
Because he served in the military, he probably
had the knowledge to be involved.

00:08:50.019 --> 00:08:56.200
His tangential past connections to suspected
terrorists were further proof.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:02.970
And since he’d converted to Islam, he checked
almost every box on the FBI’s profile list.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:05.870
And remember Mayfield’s house being searched?

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:12.410
Evidence was seized from his residence, including
what the FBI called “Spanish documents.”

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:16.890
They turned out to be his son’s Spanish
homework.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:18.860
Okay, okay, okay.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:25.899
But why did Mayfield’s own defense experts
agree with the FBI that his prints were a

00:09:25.899 --> 00:09:26.899
match?

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:32.240
They both claimed there was an “unusual
similarity” between the two prints, with

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:36.620
the Office of the Inspector General noting
that they considered the coincidence to be

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:39.959
“extraordinarily rare.”

00:09:39.959 --> 00:09:45.829
And now we’re back to people being accused
of crimes based on hypothetical low probability

00:09:45.829 --> 00:09:51.290
-- just like Sally Clark’s tragic case in
my last video.

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:57.560
The FBI deliberately manipulated their conclusions
by ignoring that the LF-17 print was only

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:03.590
a slight match with Mayfield’s -- and then
they focused on evidence that supported Mayfield’s

00:10:03.590 --> 00:10:07.090
guilt and ignored the parts that didn’t.

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:13.320
But the FBI’s bad judgment was also a function
of a weird problem we have when we get better

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:16.339
at collecting and using data.

00:10:16.339 --> 00:10:21.820
Think of crime in Galton’s era -- there
was essentially no real information to work

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:22.820
from.

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:26.010
It was very difficult to verify a suspect’s
identity.

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:32.680
It was hard to find a suspect given that there
was no video evidence, hardly any photography,

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:35.110
and primitive forensics at best.

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:41.420
It would be generations before we could begin
to collect and process things like blood types

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:42.950
and DNA.

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:46.350
The FBI didn’t have too little data.

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:49.389
They actually had too much.

00:10:49.389 --> 00:10:55.130
They knew every little detail about Brandon
Mayfield’s life, from his browser history

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:57.670
to what was in his garbage.

00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:02.850
The sheer volume of information made it easy
for them to find exactly what they were looking

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:05.779
for and only what they were looking for.

00:11:05.779 --> 00:11:10.710
Here’s an example of how that data and knowledge
gap quickly goes wrong.

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:12.290
Swedish power lines.

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:19.500
In 1992, Swedish researchers concluded a 25-year
longitudinal study to determine whether living

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:23.570
near high-voltage power lines made people
sick.

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:30.329
They studied the rates of over 800 different
illnesses and found that childhood leukemia

00:11:30.329 --> 00:11:34.650
was 4 times more common near power lines.

00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:36.930
Their data wasn’t wrong.

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:43.019
The problem was... by measuring so many variables,
they basically guaranteed that at least ONE

00:11:43.019 --> 00:11:50.020
of them would be a weird statistical outlier
-- and then they used it as proof of a cause-and-effect

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:51.250
relationship.

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:54.019
You can actually visualize what this is like.

00:11:54.019 --> 00:11:55.019
Look.

00:11:55.019 --> 00:11:59.790
If I have a bag of dice and dump them all
on my table, what’s gonna happen?

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:03.170
Most of them will form a scattered pile right
under where I dump them.

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:08.120
But you KNOW that 1 or 2 of em are gonna fly
off my table, bounce on the carpet, and lodge

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:11.230
themselves under a filing cabinet 10 feet
away.

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:12.230
Why?!?

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:15.490
They’re outliers, it just happens.

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:17.560
Alright, back to Mayfield.

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:23.470
The FBI had a preconceived notion of who was
likely to have been involved in 11M.

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:28.459
They had seemingly-reliable forensic evidence
to support a suspect’s identification.

00:12:28.459 --> 00:12:35.360
They had so much information about that suspect,
all so easily available digitally, that they

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.730
could find information that perfectly fit
their profile.

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:44.070
They dismissed the high volume of exculpatory
data… like whether Brandon Mayfield was

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:46.899
even in Europe before M-11.

00:12:46.899 --> 00:12:54.620
HE WASN’T. His passport had expired years
ago -- but the FBI claimed that meant Mayfield

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:57.630
must’ve traveled under another identity.

00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:02.040
For them, the absence of proof was proof.

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:08.259
Mayfield was detained for 2 weeks as a “material
witness” -- NOT arrested as an actual suspect,

00:13:08.259 --> 00:13:12.570
since holding him as a material witness required
far less evidence.

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:18.630
The FBI kept collecting information they thought
proved Mayfield’s guilt -- but everything

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:23.050
came crashing down when Spanish authorities
announced that they’d found the person who

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:26.040
was actually involved in 11M.

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:32.490
They matched the LF-17 print to those of an
Algerian terrorist, and they were right.

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:39.240
The FBI’s experts agreed that these prints
were truly a match.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:44.180
Mayfield was released immediately -- and then
the questions began.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:49.250
As the American Civil Liberties Union and
other civil rights advocates excoriated the

00:13:49.250 --> 00:13:56.009
FBI for having wrongly profiled him, the FBI
analyzed what went wrong internally.

00:13:56.009 --> 00:14:01.190
They laid part of the blame on what they saw
as the poor quality of the original digital

00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:07.950
image of LF-17, but a panel of international
fingerprint experts suggested that the FBI

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:13.880
had far too much confidence in the Integrated
Automated Fingerprint Identification System,

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:20.009
which is the largest biometric database in
the world, and let the pressure of a high

00:14:20.009 --> 00:14:26.319
profile case determine how they viewed the
evidence -- and what evidence they collected

00:14:26.319 --> 00:14:27.550
at all.

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:35.730
The panel also concluded that, “FBI culture
discouraged fingerprint examiners from disagreeing

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:37.610
with their superiors.”

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:43.300
The Department of Justice released a 330-page
report that admitted to analytical and procedural

00:14:43.300 --> 00:14:48.860
errors, and academic journals churned out
articles analyzing the validity of fingerprint

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:55.510
analysis, the rate of false positives, and
fingerprinting’s role in criminal trials.

00:14:55.510 --> 00:15:00.339
Regarding the validity of fingerprinting,
a science historian at UC-Irvine said, “How

00:15:00.339 --> 00:15:06.509
do you explain to the court that what you’ve
been saying for 100 years was exaggerated,

00:15:06.509 --> 00:15:10.220
but you still have something meaningful to
say?”

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:15.519
Mayfield’s case led to lawsuits about potential
misuse and constitutionality of the Patriot

00:15:15.519 --> 00:15:22.940
Act, a $2 million judgment in his favor, and
a formal apology from the US Government.

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:25.820
Mayfield went back to practicing law.

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:32.120
Nearly two decades after Mayfield’s unconscionable
injustice, we have even more data and information

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:35.350
about everything -- and about everyone.

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:40.540
It’s amazing how much we can know and how
quickly we can know it.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:46.389
Everything from medical records to finding
a gem mint Charizard is way faster than it

00:15:46.389 --> 00:15:49.209
used to be, and usually more accurate.

00:15:49.209 --> 00:15:52.910
That’s generally a good thing.

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:57.450
But… sometimes all that information is a
problem.

00:15:57.450 --> 00:16:02.899
Even when it’s perfectly accurate, the way
we use it might not be.

00:16:02.899 --> 00:16:10.170
We can make good data bad, and it’s getting
even more difficult to get it right.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:15.810
Mayfield’s case highlights the complex relationship
between the objectivity of science and math

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:19.060
and the fallibility of the human mind.

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:26.879
As we advance, we find more ways to be right
-- and that also means more ways to be wrong.

00:16:26.879 --> 00:16:33.209
The not-quite-100% reliability of fingerprint
analysis may have initially failed Brandon

00:16:33.209 --> 00:16:42.519
Mayfield, but the unchecked flaws of the human
mind are what landed him in a jail cell.

00:16:42.519 --> 00:16:50.389
Sometimes the math and science are right,
and the way we use them, the way people use

00:16:50.389 --> 00:16:53.259
what’s right... is wrong.

00:16:53.259 --> 00:16:54.990
And as always, thanks for watching.

